north_star:
When the driver sends a legislate instruction, first inspect the project until you understand its goal and its own acceptance facts, then produce an anchor proposal. Once your proposal passes the machine gate, the driver stores it on the governance server and locks it: locked means it cannot change while in force.

Why this tool is worth using well: the anchor is external memory — on a long project your context may be compacted or truncated, and re-reading the anchor recovers your own plan instead of reconstructing it from fragments. It is a self-checker — the rule layer compares reported progress against your milestones and flags drift while correction is still cheap. And it is a position fix — each milestone advance is a definite, machine-checked "you are here". The anchor governs only where you are going and whether you have arrived; how you get there — methods, paths, tools — stays entirely yours.

Proposal format — a single JSON object; the driver fills in identity and lifecycle fields, you produce exactly these:

{
  "goal": "<goal statement, natural language>",
  "milestone_seq": ["1", "2", "..."],
  "milestone_defs": [
    {"no": "1", "desc": "<what this milestone delivers>", "verify": "<a check you can actually run>"}
  ]
}

- Choose as many milestones as the work actually requires (at least one); number them consecutively. milestone_defs[i].no must equal milestone_seq[i] exactly, character for character — if milestone_seq is ["1", "2"], the no fields are "1" and "2" (not "M1", "M2"); a "M1"-style prefix is allowed only when milestone_seq itself uses that same form.
- The last milestone's verify must lead to the project's own acceptance facts: the concrete, checkable evidence that defines "done" for this project.
- The content of each verify is yours to write: you know the concrete situation best, so write checks you can actually perform. Once the anchor is locked, the machine holds you to exactly what you wrote.
- Write goal and verify texts as plain natural language: never include literal credential assignments (such as `password: <value>` or `api_key=<value>`); if the task involves credentials, refer to them indirectly (for example, "the configured password authenticates successfully"). Literal key=value credential shapes are rejected by the governance scan before publishing.
- Return the proposal JSON as your reply to the legislate instruction. If the gate rejects it, the rejection reasons are returned to you; fix and resubmit (at most three attempts).

Example (short, programming domain — adding a --json output mode to a CLI tool):

{
  "goal": "Add a --json output mode to the CLI tool so downstream consumers can parse results reliably.",
  "milestone_seq": ["1", "2", "3"],
  "milestone_defs": [
    {"no": "1", "desc": "Flag parsing and JSON serialization for the main result object.", "verify": "run the tool with --json on the sample input; exit code 0 and the output loads via json.load"},
    {"no": "2", "desc": "All subcommands emit JSON under the flag; errors become structured objects.", "verify": "each subcommand run with --json pipes clean through json.tool"},
    {"no": "3", "desc": "Tests cover the new mode and usage docs describe it.", "verify": "the project's own test suite passes, including the new JSON-mode tests"}
  ]
}
